Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'mixture of experts language model 2024 OR 2025', 'max_results': 8} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 3717 tokens): - Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (Muhammad Maaz; Hanoona Rasheed; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan)
  - Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs can be persuasive yet logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visual evidence.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces Think-Answer Consistency (TAC) to assess alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs textual cues.
  - Findings: Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, current models lean heavily on linguistic priors over visual content.
  - Method: Timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning combined with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) guided by a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR), encouraging temporally aligned, causally coherent reasoning.
  - Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; improved temporal precision and reasoning coherence yield more trustworthy video understanding.
  - Resources: Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Hanoona Rasheed; Mohammed Zumri; Muhammad Maaz; Ming-Hsuan Yang; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan)
  - Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” in text after a single encode, creating a semantic bottleneck and shallow reasoning.
  - Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—models “think with videos” by iteratively manipulating visual input.
  - Model: Video-CoM executes a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) to gather/refine evidence.
  - Data: Video-CoM Instruct—18K instruction-tuning samples for multi-step manipulation reasoning.
  - Training: Reinforcement learning with reasoning-aware GRPO and step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks; +3.6% average over recent SOTA using only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO samples; ablations show improved accuracy and interpretability.
  - Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (Bao Shu; Yan Cai; Jianjian Sun; et al.)
  - Goal: Enable LLM agents to internalize world models efficiently without rigid, prescriptive reasoning processes.
  - Approach (WMAct): Active reasoning through interaction (“doing”), not fixed chains of thought.
  - Mechanisms: (1) Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and promote purposeful interaction; (2) Interaction frequency annealing to gradually cap max interaction turns, forcing internalization of dynamics.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, WMAct solves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple interactions; shows strong transfer and boosts on broader reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhizhou Zhong; Yicheng Ji; Zhe Kong; et al.)
  - Problem: Audio-driven multi-person talking video generation is hindered by costly data collection and difficulty in coordinating multiple identities coherently.
  - Architecture: Extensible multi-stream Diffusion Transformer with a novel identity-aware attention that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary scaling of drivable identities.
  - Training Pipeline: Learns multi-person speaking patterns using only single-person videos; refines interactivity with a small number of real multi-person clips.
  - Evaluation: Introduces a targeted metric and dataset for naturalness and interactivity of multi-person generation.
  - Results: Achieves strong lip synchronization, visual quality, and natural interactivity, balancing data cost and identity scalability.

