Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2407.10671'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 183863 tokens): Title: Qwen2 Technical Report
Link: https://huggingface.co/papers/2407.10671

What Qwen2 is
- A new series of open-weight large language models (LLMs) and large multimodal models (LMMs).
- Released as a comprehensive suite of foundational and instruction-tuned models spanning 0.5B to 72B parameters, including both dense and Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) variants.
- Outperforms most prior open-weight models (including Qwen1.5) and is competitive with proprietary models across language understanding, generation, multilingual tasks, coding, mathematics, and reasoning.

Flagship performance
- Base model (Qwen2-72B):
  - MMLU 84.2
  - GPQA 37.9
  - HumanEval 64.6
  - GSM8K 89.5
  - BBH 82.4
- Instruction-tuned (Qwen2-72B-Instruct):
  - MT-Bench 9.1
  - Arena-Hard 48.1
  - LiveCodeBench 35.7

Multilingual capability
- Strong performance across ~30 languages, including English, Chinese, Spanish, French, German, Arabic, Russian, Korean, Japanese, Thai, Vietnamese, and more.

Availability and ecosystem
- Open model weights on Hugging Face and ModelScope; examples and supplementary materials on GitHub.
- Resources provided for quantization, fine-tuning, and deployment.
- Broad tooling compatibility (e.g., Transformers, Text Generation Inference, endpoints), with deployment options including Azure.
- Licenses include Apache-2.0 and “other,” depending on checkpoint.

Notable released checkpoints (IDs as listed)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct (code-focused, instruct)
- Qwen/QwQ-32B-Preview (preview of reasoning-oriented variant, based on Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (general instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct (general instruct, largest instruct in set)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct (code-focused, instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (lightweight instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (smallest instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (compact instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B (smallest base)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct (mid-large instruct)
- Qwen/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct (mid-scale instruct)

Key takeaways
- Qwen2 advances open-weight LLM/LMM quality across diverse domains and languages.
- The 72B models set strong baselines on widely used benchmarks.
- The release covers a broad size spectrum to support varied compute budgets and applications, with code and deployment tooling to ease adoption.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2403.03206'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 102946 tokens): Title: Scaling Rectified Flow Transformers for High-Resolution Image Synthesis
URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2403.03206
ArXiv ID: 2403.03206 (from tags; field empty in metadata)
Authors: Patrick Esser, Sumith Kulal, Andreas Blattmann, Rahim Entezari, Jonas Müller, Harry Saini, Yam Levi, Dominik Lorenz, Axel Sauer, Frederic Boesel, Dustin Podell, Tim Dockhorn, Zion English, Kyle Lacey, Alex Goodwin, Yannik Marek, Robin Rombach

Problem and context
- Diffusion models generate data by reversing a noise process; they dominate generative modeling for images/videos.
- Rectified Flow (RF) connects data and noise along straight lines, offering cleaner theory and simplicity but is not yet standard.

Key contributions
1) Training improvements for Rectified Flow
   - Introduce noise sampling biased toward perceptually relevant scales.
   - Large-scale study shows this training strategy yields superior performance vs. established diffusion formulations for high-resolution text-to-image synthesis.

2) New Transformer architecture for text-to-image
   - Uses separate weights for text and image modalities.
   - Enables bidirectional information flow between image and text tokens.
   - Improves text comprehension, typography, and human preference ratings.

3) Scaling behavior and validation correlation
   - Architecture follows predictable scaling trends.
   - Lower validation loss correlates with better text-to-image results across automated metrics and human evaluations.

4) Results and openness
   - Largest models outperform state-of-the-art for high-resolution text-to-image.
   - Authors will release experimental data, code, and model weights.

Implications
- Strengthens the case for rectified flow as a practical alternative to standard diffusion, especially for high-res T2I.
- Architectural changes directly improve alignment between text prompts and generated images (e.g., typography).
- Provides clear scaling laws linking validation loss to human-perceived quality.

