Takeaway
- The work advances rectified flow training via perceptually-aware noise sampling and introduces a modality-separated, bidirectionally-attentive Transformer that scales predictably and boosts text fidelity and human preference—setting new state of the art for high-resolution text-to-image and backed by released models and tooling.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2406.17557'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 174293 tokens): Title: The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale (arXiv:2406.17557)
Authors: Guilherme Penedo; Hynek Kydlíček; Loubna Ben Allal; Anton Lozhkov; Margaret Mitchell; Colin Raffel; Leandro von Werra; Thomas Wolf
URL: https://huggingface.co/papers/2406.17557

Core contributions and findings
- Introduces FineWeb, a 15-trillion token English-centric web dataset built from 96 Common Crawl snapshots, designed to improve pretraining quality at scale.
- Systematically documents all design choices and conducts extensive ablations, especially around deduplication and filtering strategies, to understand what drives data quality and downstream model performance.
- Releases FineWeb-Edu, a 1.3-trillion token educational subset filtered from FineWeb, curated to emphasize knowledge- and reasoning-rich content.
- Performance: Models pretrained on FineWeb outperform those trained on other open pretraining datasets; models pretrained on FineWeb-Edu show large gains on knowledge/reasoning benchmarks such as MMLU and ARC.
- Open resources: Public release of the datasets (FineWeb and FineWeb-Edu), the full data curation codebase, and all models trained during ablation studies to enable reproducibility and further research.

What’s in the datasets
- FineWeb: 15T tokens, aggregated from 96 Common Crawl snapshots; curated via robust deduplication and filtering pipelines.
- FineWeb-Edu: 1.3T tokens focused on high-quality educational material, filtered from FineWeb to boost knowledge-intensive reasoning capabilities.

Why it matters
- High-quality, large-scale pretraining data is a decisive factor in LLM performance, but top open models’ datasets are typically undisclosed. FineWeb provides a transparent, well-documented, and high-performing alternative, with thorough ablations to guide best practices.

Implementations referencing FineWeb/FineWeb-Edu (selected)
- BSC-LT models leveraging FineWeb-Edu among other corpora (Apache-2.0):
  - BSC-LT/ALIA-40b (40B LLM; tags include dataset:HuggingFaceFW/fineweb-edu; multi-language tags; uses transformers).
  - BSC-LT/salamandra-7b and BSC-LT/salamandra-7b-instruct (base and instruct variants).
  - BSC-LT/salamandra-2b and BSC-LT/salamandra-2b-instruct (base and instruct variants).
- NVIDIA Nemocurator educational classifiers:
  - nvidia/nemocurator-fineweb-nemotron-4-edu-classifier.
  - nvidia/nemocurator-fineweb-mixtral-edu-classifier.
- AxiomLab “Cable” positional encoding experiments:
  - axiomlaborg/Cable (and Cable Wiki/EduFineWeb tiny variants), some trained/evaluated on FineWeb-Edu or WikiText, exploring length extrapolation/context-aware encodings.
- Community and smaller models referencing FineWeb-Edu:
  - fraserlove/gpt-alpha (GPT-2 based).
  - Community GGUF/quantized ports of Salamandra (e.g., RichardErkhov and robbiemu repos) referencing the paper/dataset.

Availability
- Datasets: FineWeb (15T), FineWeb-Edu (1.3T).
- Code: Full data curation codebase public.
- Models: All ablation-trained models released for reproducibility.

Key takeaway
- FineWeb sets a new bar for open pretraining data by combining scale (15T tokens), rigorous curation (comprehensive deduplication/filtering ablations), and transparent release, while FineWeb-Edu specifically enhances knowledge and reasoning performance, making it a strong choice for pretraining LLMs aimed at academic and reasoning-heavy tasks.
Tool `Hugging Face:get-paper-info` with Parameter {'arxiv_id': '2402.00159'} on Hugging Face succeeded. Result (summarized from 30267 tokens): Title: Dolma: an Open Corpus of Three Trillion Tokens for Language Model Pretraining Research (arXiv:2402.00159)

Authors: Luca Soldaini, Rodney Kinney, Akshita Bhagia, Dustin Schwenk, David Atkinson, Russell Authur, Ben Bogin, Khyathi Raghavi Chandu, Jennifer Dumas, Yanai Elazar, Valentin Hofmann, Ananya Harsh Jha, Sachin Kumar, Li (Lucy) Lucy, Xinxi Lyu, Nathan Lambert, Ian Magnusson, Jacob Morrison, Niklas Muennighoff, Aakanksha Naik, Crystal Nam, Matthew E. Peters, Abhilasha Ravichander, Kyle Richardson, Zejiang Shen, Emma Strubell, Nishant Subramani, Oyvind Tafjord, Pete Walsh, Luke Zettlemoyer, Noah A. Smith, Hannaneh Hajishirzi, Iz Beltagy, Dirk Groeneveld, Jesse Dodge, Kyle Lo

Core contribution
- Released Dolma, an open, English corpus of 3 trillion tokens for transparent and reproducible language model pretraining research.
- Provides an open-source data curation toolkit that enables reproducing the corpus and experimenting with data construction choices.

Motivation
- Most high-performing LMs do not disclose training data, hindering research on how pretraining data impacts capabilities and limitations.
- Dolma aims to enable rigorous, open investigations into data composition, quality, and their effects on model behavior.

Corpus composition
- Diverse multi-source mixture, including:
  - General web content
  - Scientific papers
  - Code
  - Public-domain books
  - Social media
  - Encyclopedic materials
- Language: English
- Scale: ~3 trillion tokens

Documentation and methodology
- The report details design principles, construction pipeline, and a summary of contents.
- Includes analyses from training LMs on intermediate Dolma snapshots to study data curation choices.

Key findings and practices studied
- Role and effect of:
  - Content and quality filtering
  - Deduplication
  - Multi-source mixing ratios
- Results guide best practices for building pretraining corpora and highlight trade-offs among filtering, diversity, and scale.

Impact and usage
- Dolma was used to train OLMo, a state-of-the-art, fully open language model and framework for studying the science of language modeling.
- The open toolkit and corpus support replication and further experimentation by the community.

Implementations and related releases
- Multiple experimental checkpoints and datasets released on Hugging Face (author: allenai), supporting the paper’s data-decisions research (DataDecide series).
- Example model families (parameter sizes from ~6M to 1B) and mixtures include:
  - DataDecide-dolma1_7-* (e.g., no-reddit-1B, no-math-code-750M, 90M)
  - DataDecide-c4-* (e.g., 16M, 750M, 6M)
  - DataDecide-fineweb-* (e.g., edu-10M/14M, pro-8M)
  - DataDecide-falcon-and-cc-qc-* (e.g., orig-10p-4M, 20p-150M, tulu variants)
  - DCLM baseline variants with quality control and FineWeb mixes
- Tags indicate use of OLMo (hf_olmo), Apache-2.0 licensing for model artifacts, and replication focus for data decisions.

Availability
- Paper page: https://huggingface.co/papers/2402.00159
- Open-source data curation toolkit and released models support reproducibility and further research on pretraining data design.
