  Title: Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models
  Authors: Muhammad Maaz; Hanoona Rasheed; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
  Field: cs.CV | Source: arXiv | Date: 2025-11-28
  Links: abs http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23478v1 | PDF https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23478v1
  Key points:
  - Problem: Current video “thinking” models often produce plausible but logically inconsistent or weakly visual-grounded reasoning.
  - Diagnostics introduced:
    - Think Answer Consistency (TAC): measures alignment between reasoning and final answers.
    - Video Attention Score (VAS): measures reliance on visual evidence vs textual priors.
  - Finding: Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, models rely heavily on linguistic priors over visual content.
  - Method: Two-stage post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + GRPO with a novel Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to improve temporal precision and causal coherence.
  - Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks, yielding more accurate, trustworthy video understanding.
  - Availability: Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

- Paper ID: 2511.23477v1
  Title: Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations
  Authors: Hanoona Rasheed; Mohammed Zumri; Muhammad Maaz; Ming-Hsuan Yang; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
  Field: cs.CV | Source: arXiv | Date: 2025-11-28
  Links: abs http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23477v1 | PDF https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23477v1 | Code https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM
  Key points:
  - Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” once (static context), unable to rewatch/refocus/verify, limiting spatio-temporal reasoning.
  - Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—models “think with videos” by treating video as an active workspace.
  - Model: Video-CoM performs a Chain of Manipulations (CoM), executing iterative visual actions to gather/refine evidence.
  - Data: Video-CoM Instruct, an 18K instruction-tuning set for multi-step manipulation reasoning.
  - RL: Reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards) to encourage grounded, consistent reasoning.
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks; +3.6% average over recent SOTA while training on only ~25K SFT and 3K GRPO video samples. Ablations show reasoning-aware rewards improve accuracy and interpretability.

- Paper ID: 2511.23476v1
  Title: Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction
  Authors: Bao Shu; Yan Cai; Jianjian Sun; Chunrui Han; En Yu; Liang Zhao; Jingcheng Hu; Yinmin Zhang; Haoran Lv; Yuang Peng; Zheng Ge; Xiangyu Zhang; Daxin Jiang; Xiangyu Yue
  Field: cs.AI | Source: arXiv | Date: 2025-11-28
  Links: abs http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23476v1 | PDF https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.23476v1
  Key points:
  - Goal: Develop robust, transferable world-model reasoning for LLM agents via efficient interaction and active learning.
  - Approach (WMAct): Removes rigid, structured reasoning to let the model “shape thinking through doing.”
    - Reward rescaling: Adjusts outcome rewards by action efficacy to reduce redundancy and incentivize purposeful interaction.
    - Interaction frequency annealing: Progressively reduces the max allowed interaction turns, pushing internalization of environment dynamics.
  - Benchmarks: Sokoban, Maze, Taxi.
  - Results: Achieves single-turn solutions for tasks that previously required multiple interactions; strong transfer to complex environments; improved performance on a suite of reasoning benchmarks.

