  - Authors: Junshu Tang; Jiacheng Liu; Jiaqi Li; Longhuang Wu; Haoyu Yang; Penghao Zhao; Siruis Gong; Xiang Yuan; Shuai Shao; Qinglin Lu
  - Problem: Existing generative game world models rely on rigid action schemas and costly annotations, limiting diverse, player-driven interactions.
  - Approach: Introduces instruction-driven interaction where users control generated game video via natural language, keyboard, or mouse. Formally defines “interactive video data” and automates conversion of large-scale unstructured text–video pairs into causally aligned interactive datasets.
  - Model: Built on a 14B image-to-video Mixture-of-Experts foundation with a text-driven interaction injection mechanism for fine-grained control of camera motion, character behavior, and environment dynamics.
  - Benchmark: InterBench for comprehensive interaction evaluation.
  - Results: Generates temporally coherent, causally grounded interactive game videos that reliably follow free-form instructions (e.g., “open the door,” “draw a torch,” “trigger an explosion”).
  - Links: arXiv abs/pdf

- La ley del descenso tendencial de la tasa de ganancia: Evidencia empírica para la economía española (econ.GN; arXiv:2511.23427v1, 2025-11-28)
  - Author: Iván López-Espejo
  - Scope: Tests Marx’s law of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall in Spain (1960–2024), focusing on organic composition of capital and rate of surplus value.
  - Method: Transforms Spanish National Accounts (BDMACRO) into Marxist variables—constant capital (c), variable capital (v), surplus value (pv)—and builds historical series for organic composition (q), rate of surplus value (pv′), and profit rate (g′), all at constant prices.
  - Findings: Sustained increase in q and a slight decrease in pv′ produce a tendential decline in g′, with cyclical fluctuations linked to crises.
  - Conclusion: Empirical support for the law in Spain; highlights structural limits of capitalism.
  - Links: arXiv abs/pdf

- LFM2 Technical Report (cs.LG; cs.AI; arXiv:2511.23404v1, 2025-11-28)
  - Authors: Alexander Amini et al.
  - Goal: Liquid Foundation Models optimized for on-device deployment with strong task performance.
  - Architecture: Hardware-in-the-loop search under edge latency/memory constraints yields a compact hybrid backbone combining gated short convolutions with few grouped query attention blocks; up to 2x faster prefill/decode on CPUs vs similarly sized models.
  - Family: 350M–8.3B parameters; dense (350M, 700M, 1.2B, 2.6B) and MoE (8.3B total, 1.5B active); 32K context.
  - Training: 10–12T tokens; tempered, decoupled Top-K knowledge distillation; curriculum with difficulty-ordered data; three-stage post-training (SFT, length-normalized preference optimization, model merging).
  - Performance: LFM2-2.6B: 79.56% IFEval, 82.41% GSM8K.
  - Variants: LFM2-VL (token-efficient vision-language with tunable accuracy-latency), LFM2-Audio (separate audio I/O paths for real-time speech-to-speech competitive with 3x larger models), LFM2-ColBERT (low-latency encoder for multi-lingual retrieval).
  - Release: Open weights; deployment for ExecuTorch, llama.cpp, vLLM.
  - Links: arXiv abs/pdf

