Across randomized trials, meta-analyses, and pragmatic studies, real-time AI/computer-aided detection (CADe) during colonoscopy generally increases detection of adenomas, polyps, and sessile serrated lesions, though effects on advanced adenomas and real-world ADR can be inconsistent. Evidence is growing on how CADe performs with adjunctive techniques, under varying bowel prep quality, in training settings, and potential endoscopist behavior changes. New AI-driven quality metrics and strategies (e.g., mucosal exposure optimization, optical diagnosis/leave-in-situ) are being evaluated, and large screening-focused RCTs are underway.

Key studies and findings

- Jeon et al. (Prospective multicenter RCT)
  • Aim: Evaluate real-time CADe efficacy for colorectal neoplasm detection.
  • Outcome data not reported in abstract.

- Schauer et al. “Man Plus Machine” (RCT)
  • Aim: Assess CADe combined with adjunctive detection techniques (longer withdrawal, cecal/rectal retroflexion, dynamic position change, NBI).

- Sattar et al. (Systematic review/meta-analysis of GI Genius; 7 RCTs; n=9,639)
  • ADR: RR 1.12 (95% CI 1.03–1.22; I²=64%).
  • PDR: RR 1.05 (95% CI 1.01–1.10; I²=0%).
  • SSL detection: RR 1.27 (95% CI 1.11–1.47; I²=11%).
  • Lesion counts: adenomas/procedure SMD 0.15 (95% CI 0.09–0.20; I²=29%); polyps/procedure SMD 0.10 (95% CI 0.05–0.15; I²=0%).
  • Advanced ADR: no difference (RR 1.01; 95% CI 0.90–1.13; I²=6%).
  • Limited safety reporting; supports routine GI Genius use with training and adherence to alerts; calls for larger standardized trials with long-term endpoints.

- Tan et al. (Systematic review/network meta-analysis)
  • Scope: CADe/CADx effects on adenoma detection and optical histology; notes ongoing uncertainty.

- Xu BX et al.
  • Topic: Personalizing withdrawal time based on insertion time to reach target ADR; underscores procedural factor variability.

- Sultany et al. (Systematic review/meta-analysis)
  • Aim: Real-time AI vs standard colonoscopy for early CRC detection.

- Wang D et al. (Meta-analysis with trial sequential analysis)
  • Aim: Size-stratified effectiveness of AI on ADR and adenoma miss rate (AMR) across adenoma sizes.

- Chang PW et al. (Pragmatic RCT in GI fellowship training)
  • Aim: Impact of AI-assisted colonoscopy on trainee performance, addressing right-sided miss rates.

- Beran et al. (Meta-analysis)
  • Question: Added value of combining AI with mucosal exposure devices (e.g., Endocuff) versus AI alone for neoplasia detection.

- Muccichini et al.
  • Aim: Influence of bowel preparation quality (levels within “adequate” prep) on CADe effectiveness.

- Antonelli et al. PRACTICE (Non-inferiority RCT)
  • Topic: Safety of AI-assisted optical diagnosis to leave ≤5 mm rectosigmoid polyps in situ when predicted non-neoplastic; first RCT to test leave-in-situ strategy with AI.

- Li et al. (Multicenter prospective observational; AI quality indicator)
  • Developed CCMEA (cumulative colorectal mucosal exposure area) using ResNet50 + UNet++.
  • Threshold 2000 set around ADR of 25%.
  • n=510; “qualified” (≥2000) vs “unqualified” colonoscopies: ADR 53.7% vs 14.2%; adjusted OR 8.0 (95% CI 5.0–12.8).
  • Suggests AI-derived mucosal exposure metric strongly associates with higher ADR.

- Xu X et al. (Prospective multicenter randomized, parallel-controlled trial)
  • Aim: Efficacy and accuracy of a real-time CADe system to reduce ≈30% miss rate of polyps/adenomas.

- Xu AA et al. (Pragmatic implementation at trainee-dominated county hospital)
  • Aim: Real-world impact of CADe rollout on colonoscopy quality metrics; addresses concerns from prior pragmatic studies.

- Kumar et al. (Systematic review/network meta-analysis)
  • Aim: Compare effectiveness across different AI systems for colorectal neoplasia detection.

- Budzyń et al. (Multicenter observational)
  • Topic: Endoscopist deskilling risk after regular AI exposure; evaluates performance when AI is not in use.

- Cheng et al. (Tw
