- Identification and validation of shared diagnostic markers for sepsis-induced ARDS and cardiomyopathy: Used WGCNA and machine learning to discover and validate common markers and explore mechanisms linking these sepsis complications.

- Neonatal mortality in Lusaka, Zambia: Compared traditional survival analysis with machine learning to identify predictors of neonatal death (Jan 2018–Sep 2019); aimed to assess factors and predictive performance in a high-burden setting.

- S100A12 as a key biomarker for sepsis: scRNA-seq and 113 ML frameworks identified neutrophil expansion in sepsis; 13 neutrophil-associated DEGs overlapped; a 4-gene Random Forest model (S100A12, PIK3AP1, HLA-DMB, RETN) achieved the top mean C-index and strong ROC/calibration/DCA; SHAP ranked S100A12 highest; theophylline, aspirin, and aminophylline flagged as potential compounds.

- AI-CDSS for bacteremia (OneChoice/OneChoice Fusion): Surveyed 65 specialists across 90 AI recommendations using clinical vignettes; evaluated perceived utility, impact on judgment, concordance, and validity of therapy changes across diverse pathogens.

- Multi-agent systems for perioperative care: Perspective on MAS orchestrating data-to-decisions for safer, personalized perioperative management.

- Addressing class imbalance in sepsis mortality prediction: Proposed a full region synthetic sampling approach to improve generalization of ML models on imbalanced ICU datasets.

- ML mortality prediction for V-A ECMO: Multicenter cohort (n=280; two internal sources + external site). Logistic regression outperformed complex models: internal AUC 0.86 (accuracy 0.76; sensitivity 0.73; specificity 0.79; F1 0.73), external AUC 0.75 (accuracy 0.69). Best calibration (lowest Brier 0.1496) and net benefit on DCA. SHAP: lactate and age (+), albumin (−). Stronger in cardiac arrest (AUC 0.81), non-sepsis (0.75), non-diabetes (0.78).

- AI-assisted design against 16S rRNA methyltransferase: Explores doxycycline-aided AI design targeting resistance-driving methyltransferase implicated in UTI, GI infections, sepsis, pneumonia; underscores need for novel therapeutics.

- Early prediction of sepsis-induced coagulopathy: Multi-center retrospective study using interpretable ML to anticipate SIC onset; focuses on transparent risk drivers.

- Intestinal immune network for IgA in sepsis: Multi-omics indicates this pathway is involved in sepsis development and progression, linking gut barrier injury to systemic dysregulation.

- Protein biomarkers for Gram-negative vs Gram-positive sepsis: Identified adult blood protein markers to rapidly differentiate pathogen class for earlier targeted therapy.

- Wearable vital-sign sensors in low-resource settings: Advocates affordable continuous monitoring feeding ML to detect early deterioration in severe dengue and sepsis, overcoming spot-check limitations.

- Pediatric fungemia mortality prediction: In 85 PICU cases, integrating Candida Score with clinical variables; test-set AUCs: LR 0.800 (accuracy 0.735), RF 0.861 (precision 1.000; recall 0.778), GBM 0.847 (precision 0.875; recall 0.778). Internal validation supported stability; needs multicenter external validation.

- LDH-to-albumin ratio (LDAR) in GI cancer ICU: Assessed LDAR as a 28-day mortality predictor and its incremental value within ML models using MIMIC-IV.

- Butyrate metabolism in sepsis: Identified key BM-related genes potentially linked to sepsis pathogenesis and therapeutic strategies.

- Angiotensin II in distributive shock: Compared conclusions from a meta-analysis with outputs from four AI tools to synthesize clinical experience post-approval (Spain, 2023).

- TCDD exposure and sepsis biomarkers: Network toxicology, transcriptomics, ML, and Mendelian randomization identified PPARG and CASPASE-3 as key biomarker
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': '(mammography OR "breast screening") AND ("artificial intelligence" OR "deep learning" OR CAD OR AI) AND (randomized OR prospective OR "reader study" OR implementation) AND ("cancer detection" OR recall OR specificity OR sensitivity) AND ("2019/01/01"[Date - Publication] : "2025/12/31"[Date - Publication])', 'max_results': 25} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 8732 tokens): Summary of key findings across included papers (AI in radiology and breast imaging)

- AI for radiology in LMICs (scoping review): Access to imaging in LMICs is constrained by equipment scarcity, geography, digital infrastructure, and workforce shortages. AI is a promising tool to boost diagnostic accuracy, support non-specialist providers, and streamline workflows. Successful adoption hinges on infrastructure readiness, validation, and local capacity building.

- Economic value of AI in radiology (systematic review, 21 studies, 2010–2024): Most studies assessed ML/DL (48%), CAD (33%), NLP (10%), and hypothetical models (10%). AI yielded cost savings when applied to resource-intensive tasks, with human-comparable accuracy and fixed-cost models (e.g., lung cancer screening savings up to USD 242/patient). Costs rose with inferior specificity or pay-per-use pricing (e.g., CAD increased mammography screening costs by up to USD 19/patient). In fast tasks (e.g., radiograph reads), AI had value in settings with radiologist shortages. Additional value from protocol optimization and improved follow-up compliance. Conclusion: economic value is context-dependent; more high-quality evaluations are needed.

- Preoperative imaging of LVI in breast cancer (review): Synthesizes ultrasound (including CEUS, elastography), mammography (digital and contrast-enhanced), MRI (DCE, DWI, IVIM), and AI (radiomics/deep learning). Performance: ultrasound sensitivity ~60–75%; MRI ~78–90%. Radiomics models show AUC 0.74–0.896 but face inconsistent feature extraction and limited external validation. Gaps: lack of standardized parameters, underrepresentation of rare subtypes, false positives from benign proliferations, false negatives from obscured lesions. Calls for multicenter prospective validation of AI-integrated multimodal workflows and consensus imaging guidelines.

- RCTs for new breast screening methods (perspective): RCTs remain the gold standard but are slow and costly relative to rapid AI development and regulatory approval, which can drive adoption absent definitive efficacy data. Proposes a middle ground leveraging alternative data (e.g., method of detection), professional initiatives, and pragmatic approaches to evaluate and integrate new technologies faster and responsibly.

- Early AI in mammography screening (study): Title indicates early results of AI use in screening; specific outcomes not provided in abstract.

- YOLO for mammography: Title indicates YOLO-based methods can improve diagnostic performance; details not provided.

- Automated software for mammography image quality and technique (scoping review): Reviews automated tools assessing positioning and compression—critical for effective screening—and their impact; details not provided.

- AI-STREAM trial secondary analysis (screening mammography with AI-CAD; 24,543 women; 148 cancers): AI-CAD positive in 6.2% (1,535/24,543), negative in 93.8%. Overall PPV1 8.7% (133/1,535), sensitivity 89.9%, specificity 94.3%. PPV rose with higher AI abnormality scores; remained <3% in low-score groups for dense breasts. AI detected 3.4% (5/148) of cancers missed by radiologists but missed 8.1% (12/148) found by radiologists. Lower AI scores were associated with mammographic asymmetry. Highlights complementary strengths and density/lesion-type effects.

