Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': '("mammography" OR "breast screening") AND ("artificial intelligence" OR "AI") AND (MASAI OR "Malmö" OR randomized OR prospective) AND (workload OR "cancer detection") AND ("2019/01/01"[Date - Publication] : "2025/12/31"[Date - Publication])', 'max_results': 25} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 8131 tokens): Summary of key findings across included studies on AI in breast cancer imaging and related diagnostics

Prospective and real‑world screening performance
- AI-STREAM (Korea; NCT05024591, prospective multicenter, single-read): 24,543 women; 140 screen-detected cancers (0.57‰). AI-CAD increased cancer detection rate (CDR) by 13.8% (5.70 vs 5.01 per 1,000; p<0.001) without increasing recall rate. Secondary analysis (24,543 women; 148 cancers after 1-year follow-up): AI positive 6.2% (1,535/24,543); PPV1 8.7% (133/1,535); sensitivity 89.9%, specificity 94.3%. PPV rose with higher AI abnormality scores; in dense breasts, low-score strata had PPV <3%. AI found 3.4% (5/148) cancers missed by radiologists but missed 8.1% (12/148) detected by radiologists; mammographic asymmetry tended to have lower AI scores.
- PRAIM (Germany; nationwide, real-world implementation, double reading): 463,094 women (260,739 with AI; 119 radiologists). AI-supported double reading achieved higher CDR: 6.7/1,000 vs 5.7/1,000 (+17.6%, 95% CI +5.7%, +30.8%), with lower, noninferior recall rate: 37.4 vs 38.3 per 1,000 (−2.5%, 95% CI −6.5%, +1.7%). PPV of recall improved (17.9% vs 14.9%); PPV of biopsy improved (64.5% vs 59.2%).
- MASAI (randomised, controlled, non-inferiority screening accuracy study): Emerging evidence supports increased detection with reduced workload; detailed metrics pending.
- Large retrospective evaluation (Norway): CE-marked AI assessed on 1,017,208 screening exams; results intended to guide safe prospective implementation (metrics not detailed here).

Human–AI interaction, reader studies, and clinical workflow
- Systematic review of reader studies (47 studies, 2010–Feb 2025): AI assistance generally improves radiologist detection/diagnosis performance and efficiency across mammography, ultrasound, and MRI. Benefit varies by task, reader experience, and integration mode; calls for stronger study designs, prospective validation, and explicit human–AI collaboration strategies.
- ScreenTrustCAD trial (prospective, three independent readers: two radiologists + AI-CAD): Among 54,991 women, 5,489 exams flagged for consensus, 1,348 recalled. When flagged by a single reader, recall proportion was higher if the flagger was a radiologist (14.2%, 263/1,858) versus AI-CAD (4.6%, 86/1,886). Findings highlight risks of over‑ or under‑reliance on AI and differences in PPV depending on who flags cases.
- Implementation risk management: At Capio S:t Göran (ScreenTrustCAD site), an enterprise risk management approach identified risks and defined mitigation actions prior to standalone AI deployment in screening.

Error analysis, false positives, and missed cancers
- High AI score but true-negative exams (BreastScreen Norway; Transpara v2.0.0): Of 54,662 exams, 2,124 with AI score 10 and subsequent true-negative outcomes were identified; 382 randomly reviewed. 91.1% were rated negative by blinded radiologists. Feature breakdown: asymmetry 30.6%, calcifications 30.1% (79.1% benign morphology), asymmetry+calcifications 29.3%, mass 8.9%, distortion 0.8%, spiculated mass 0.3%. All BI-RADS d density cases were interpreted as negative. Suggests many AI false positives are non-suspicious and might not trigger recall when AI is decision support.
- One-view DBT non-detections: Study classified true vs false negatives and analyzed reasons for missed cancers to inform DBT screening optimization.
- Reader discrepancies: Study of challenging cases examined where radiography advanced practitioners and radiologists differ, to inform training and AI model development.

Calibration, generalizability, and bias
- VAIB study (retrospective simulation): Mismatch between calibration and target populations can distort CDR substantially—by acquisition year (−3% to +19%), age (−0.2% to +27%), density (+1% to +16%), and especially vendor (−32% to +33%). Reinforces necessity of representative calibration for safe deployment.
- Broad reviews emphasize: dataset quality, population/vend
