AI for large vessel occlusion (LVO) triage and workflow
- AI platform implementation improves speed-to-treatment in real-world practice (Akhter et al., 222 EVT patients, urban center with majority Hispanic population):
  - Pre- vs post-Viz LVO (July 2018–July 2022; AI implemented July 2020; n=84 vs 138): proportion treated within 90 min rose from 26.5% (95% CI 17.9–36.8) to 68.1% (95% CI 60.0–75.5), p<0.001.
  - Median door-to-groin time fell from 109 to 75 min, p<0.001; interrupted time-series confirmed DTG reduction (p=0.001).
  - More AIS patients received EVT post-AI; no differences in TICI, length of stay, or mortality/hospice.
  - Conclusion: AI-based workflow coordination can substantially compress treatment timelines without clear short-term outcome differences.
- Systematic review/meta-analysis of Viz.ai impact (Sarhan et al., 12 studies; 15,595 patients):
  - Significant reductions: CT-to-EVT (SMD −0.71), door-to-groin (SMD −0.50), CT-to-recanalization (SMD −0.55), and door-in–door-out (SMD −0.49); all p<0.001.
  - No statistically significant differences in clinical outcomes (sICH, any ICH, mortality, 90-day mRS<2, length of stay).
- Narrative reviews (Heeralal et al.; Alqadi & Vidal): 
  - AI spans imaging (LVO, hemorrhage, perfusion), prediction (outcomes, hemorrhagic risk), and workflow orchestration (coordination, communication).
  - Some tools (RapidAI, Viz.ai) have multicenter validation; most are early-stage.
  - Key gaps/risks: dataset bias, explainability, access disparities, sparse cost-effectiveness data. AI should augment—not replace—clinical judgment; future work needs prospective multicenter validation, equity-focused deployment, standardized economic evaluations, and interpretability frameworks.
- Multicenter survey of AI LVO tools (Darkhabani et al.):
  - U.S. programs show growing adoption but heterogeneous access and integration, limiting impact; addresses usage patterns and disparities.

AI imaging and prediction innovations
- Generative AI for perfusion from non-contrast CT (Primiano et al., n=99):
  - Modified pix2pix-turbo GAN predicts rCBF and Tmax maps from NCHCT using CTP-derived ground truth (VIZ.ai).
  - Performance: Tmax SSIM 0.827, PSNR 16.99, FID 62.21; rCBF SSIM 0.79, PSNR 16.38, FID 59.58.
  - Suggests feasible cross-modality estimation that could aid settings lacking CTP.
- Deep learning on non-contrast CT to estimate initial core volume (Flores et al.):
  - Evaluates association of NCCT-derived core volume with outcomes in reperfusion-treated patients as a selection/prognostic tool.
- Post-thrombectomy imaging biomarker (Poggi et al.):
  - s-ASPECTS based on contrast staining on immediate flat-panel CT post-EVT assessed for reliability and functional outcome prediction.

