- Diabetic retinopathy (DR) is a leading cause of preventable blindness; screening uptake is limited by workforce and access gaps. Autonomous AI and teleophthalmology are being used to expand point-of-care screening and follow-up, with growing real-world evidence, implementation frameworks, and ethical guidance.

Diagnostic accuracy and clinical performance
- EyeArt (Eyenuk): Systematic review/meta-analysis (PRISMA-DTA) pooled sensitivity/specificity for referable DR (rDR) from color fundus photos using bivariate random-effects models; subgroup and applicability analyses assessed heterogeneity and clinical relevance.
- IDx-DR (Digital Diagnostics): Multiple evaluations:
  - Systematic review/meta-analysis of diagnostic accuracy for DR detection for the FDA-authorized autonomous system.
  - Real-world outpatient clinic study assessed effectiveness, safety, and feasibility of IDx-DR as a routine screening tool.
  - AI–human hybrid workflow study compared IDx-DR with human teleophthalmology over 2.5 years and tested a hybrid approach that routed referable cases for expert assessment.
- Deep learning for DME: New autonomous DL model for diabetic macular edema (DME) detection from color fundus images validated across diverse, multidevice clinical settings.
- ARIAS for DME: Study estimated sensitivity, specificity, and misclassification rates for an automated retinal image analysis system detecting active DME and identified factors linked to true/false positives.

Implementation, real-world programs, and outcomes
- Adoption lessons: Five years into commercialization, autonomous AI DR testing remains limited in uptake; a review synthesizes clinical evidence, current adoption, and themes of successful health system implementation.
- STATUS program (Northern California): Year-2 evaluation of an AI-based DR detection program in primary care/endocrinology showed improvements after targeted interventions to increase image gradability and completed encounters.
- ACCESS randomized controlled trial (youth, 8–21 years): Autonomous AI at point-of-care achieved 100% diabetic eye exam completion (95% CI 95.5%–100%) vs 22% (95% CI 14.2%–32.4%) with standard referral/education (p<0.001). Among abnormal AI results, 64% completed follow-through vs 22% in control (p<0.001).
- Northern Ontario pilot: Feasibility, implementation, and patient experience of autonomous AI-based DR detection in a regional program.
- Rural Maine (EyeArt): Patient and provider experiences and perceptions during implementation in a rural primary care setting.
- Resource-limited setting (Belize): Deployment of an autonomous AI device within the Stanford Belize Vision Clinic assessed screening utility and estimated local DM/DR prevalence in an underserved population.
- Primary care augmentation (DeepDR-LLM): Integrated image-language system combining a DR transformer and LLM:
  - LLM performed comparably to PCPs/endocrinology residents (English) and outperformed PCPs with comparable resident performance (Chinese).
  - For rDR, average PCP accuracy improved from 81.0% (unassisted) to 92.3% (assisted).
  - In a prospective deployment, newly diagnosed patients under PCP+DeepDR-LLM showed better self-management behaviors (P<0.05), and patients with rDR had higher adherence to referrals (P<0.01). Communication quality and empathy improved.
- Workflow optimization: Real-world analysis identified predictors of nondiagnostic imaging before instituting reflexive dilation (as used in the pivotal FDA De Novo trial), enabling predictive dilation strategies to improve gradability and efficiency.

Teleophthalmology and access
- Teleophthalmology-enabled devices help bridge rural care gaps by bringing portable imaging, connectivity, and remote interpretation to underserved communities, enabling earlier intervention for DR, glaucoma, and AMD.
- Prospective community screenings during COVID-19 demonstrated feasibility of deep learning applications in non-mydriatic, student-run programs
