- Early recognition and rapid, targeted treatment remain central to improving sepsis outcomes. Across settings (ICU, ED, out-of-hours primary care), innovations span rapid molecular diagnostics, biomarker panels, machine learning (ML)–driven alerting, and novel therapeutics.

Key advances and findings

1) Innovative diagnostics and biomarkers (Review; 2026; Mirmotahari et al.)
- Problem: Traditional blood culture is essential but slow (long turnaround) and less sensitive.
- Technologies: Real-time PCR, MALDI-TOF mass spectrometry, biosensor-based point-of-care platforms, and novel biomarker detection systems enable identification of pathogens and host-response markers within hours.
- Biomarkers: Procalcitonin (PCT) is most established; additional promising markers include presepsin, sTREM-1, IL-6, CXCL5, and circular RNAs (circRNAs) for diagnosis and prognosis.
- Impact: Faster, more accurate diagnosis; supports antimicrobial stewardship; potential to improve survival when integrated into clinical workflows.
- Methods: Literature review (PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science, Google Scholar) with keywords “sepsis” and “diagnostic strategies.”

2) Sepsis alert systems in Emergency Departments (Systematic Review/Meta-analysis; 2024; Kim et al.)
- Context: EDs face delays due to high patient volumes; alerts aim to accelerate care per Surviving Sepsis Campaign (SSC) guidelines.
- Scope: Evaluates effects of ED sepsis alert systems on mortality and adherence to time-sensitive sepsis care.
- Takeaway: Synthesizes evidence on whether alerts expedite diagnosis and treatment initiation; specific pooled outcomes not provided in the abstract.

3) ML-assisted real-time sepsis alert in the ED (Cluster-randomized trial; 2024; Kijpaisalratana et al.)
- Design: Adult ED patient data streamed into an ML model; intervention cluster displayed alerts when ML threshold met; control used standard alerting.
- Population: 256 (intervention) vs 318 (control) sepsis patients.
- Timeliness:
  - Antibiotics within 1 hour: 68.4% vs 60.1% (P=0.04).
  - Antibiotics within 3 hours: 94.5% vs 89.0% (P=0.02).
  - Median triage-to-antibiotic time: 46 vs 50 minutes (marginally shorter with ML).
- Diagnostic accuracy: ML AUROC exceeded qSOFA, SIRS, and MEWS for early identification.
- Conclusion: ML alerting improved antibiotic timeliness and outperformed rule-based tools; mortality impact not specified.
- Trial registration: TCTR20230120001 (retrospective; 16 Jan 2023).

4) Predicting sepsis-related deterioration from telephone triage (Cohort study; 2023; Loots et al.)
- Setting: Out-of-hours GP cooperatives assessing patients with acute infections.
- Aim: Use telephone triage information to predict sepsis-related mortality and ICU admission, aiding decisions about immediate hospital referral while avoiding unnecessary transfers.
- Implication: Potential for risk stratification from prehospital triage data; detailed model performance not included in the abstract.

5) Lipid emulsion therapy to stabilize cholesterol in sepsis (Phase I/II protocol; 2019; Guirgis et al.)
- Rationale: HDL and LDL may protect in sepsis; early sepsis is associated with critically low cholesterol levels linked to poor outcomes.
- Intervention: FDA-approved fish oil–containing anti-inflammatory lipid emulsion (used in parenteral nutrition) to provide substrate for cholesterol synthesis and modulate inflammation.
- Objective: First pilot trial (LIPIDS-P) to test feasibility and biological effect of stabilizing early cholesterol levels in sepsis/septic shock.
- Status: Protocol; no efficacy results reported.

6) EHR-based clinical decision support (CDS) sepsis alert (Randomized evaluation; 2019; Downing et al.)
- Context: CDS for sepsis has shown mixed results due to heterogeneous populations, methods, and interventions.
- Study: Randomized evaluation of an EHR-based severe sepsis alert.
- Implication: Contributes evidence on effectiveness of EHR alerts; detailed outcomes not provided in 
