Overview across women’s cancers
- Rockall et al. (2025, Series): AI is rapidly impacting the full cancer care continuum for women—from early detection via digital phenotyping to diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up. Clinically translated areas include AI-assisted mammographic screening and AI-based contouring in radiotherapy, which streamlines planning and improves consistency. Active research spans radiomics for ovarian tumor characterization, machine learning for endometrial cancer subtyping, and automated cervical cancer screening. Major challenges: data scarcity, tumor heterogeneity in gynecological cancers, and lack of large, prospective validation cohorts. Generative and multimodal AI could mitigate data limitations by leveraging diverse, large-scale datasets. Trust requires rigorous real-world prospective validation, regulatory oversight, and legal frameworks. There is an opportunity to develop inclusive, clinically meaningful AI devices for all women’s cancers through multidisciplinary collaboration and national/international innovation initiatives, with the goal of earlier diagnoses and personalized treatments.

Breast imaging and screening with AI

Randomized trials and clinical implementation
- Hernström et al. (2025): MASAI trial on screening performance and cancer characteristics in AI-supported mammography screening. Randomized, controlled, parallel-group, non-inferiority, single-blinded screening accuracy study aimed at further understanding clinical impact. Emerging evidence suggests AI can raise detection and reduce reading workload.
- Lång et al. (2023): MASAI clinical safety analysis. Randomized, controlled, non-inferiority, single-blinded screening accuracy study comparing AI-supported screen reading vs standard double reading by radiologists; primary focus on clinical safety.

Workload optimization, reading strategies, and triage
- Dahlblom et al. (2025): Simulation study using AI with digital breast tomosynthesis (DBT) and synthetic mammography (SM). SM (generated from DBT) is faster to read; the study evaluates AI-driven stratification of cases to SM or DBT to minimize workload while maximizing accuracy.
- Dahlblom et al. (2023): Retrospective comparison of different AI-implemented reading strategies for DBT to address increased workload while preserving improved cancer detection relative to digital mammography (DM).
- Lång et al. (2021): AI for identifying normal mammograms in a large screening population, with the goal of reducing unnecessary reads and workload.

Selective and personalized use of DBT
- Dahlblom et al. (2023): Personalized screening framework where AI selects women who would benefit most from DBT, recognizing DBT’s higher sensitivity but greater resource demands compared with DM.

Understanding limitations and failure modes
- Johnson et al. (2025): Analysis of breast cancers not detected in one-view DBT screening. Classifies non-detected cancers into true vs false negatives, compares to true positives, and investigates reasons for non-detection to inform improvements.

Translational projects and programmatic implementation
- Tingberg et al. (2025): The Malmö Breast Tomosynthesis Screening Project—chronicles the journey from inception through current state and future plans for implementing DBT in population screening.

AI for therapy response assessment
- Förnvik et al. (2024): Prospective cohort study leveraging deep learning on serial DBT during neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NACT) to predict pathological complete response (pCR) after NACT.

AI performance relative to DBT-ground truth
- Dahlblom et al. (2021): Evaluates an AI system on DM in a screening population with ground truth defined by DBT, asking whether AI can detect cancers on DM that were originally only detected at DBT.

