These papers collectively evaluate autonomous and assistive AI for diabetic eye disease—primarily fundus-based screening for referable diabetic retinopathy (rDR) and diabetic macular edema (DME)—across accuracy, real-world deployment, workflow optimization, adherence/equity impact, hybrid human-AI models, and ethics. They also include AI-in-ophthalmology reviews, DME treatment, biomarker discovery, and structure–function studies.

Diagnostic accuracy and validation
- EyeArt (autonomous AI): Systematic review/meta-analysis (Wang 2025) following PRISMA-DTA pooled sensitivity/specificity for EyeArt detecting rDR from color fundus photographs using bivariate random-effects models, with subgroup/applicability analyses to assess heterogeneity and clinical relevance.
- IDx-DR (autonomous AI): Systematic review/meta-analysis (Khan 2025) evaluating diagnostic accuracy for DR detection.
- Head-to-head/real-world algorithm comparison: Poschkamp 2025 compared two non-mydriatic AI tools (IDx-DR classification; Thirona RetCAD regression) against mydriatic ophthalmologist reference, and assessed a customized referral threshold (“Greifswald modification”) to adapt to local needs and image quality constraints.
- Outpatient effectiveness/safety: Huber 2025 assessed IDx-DR’s efficacy, safety, and feasibility in routine clinics.
- AI-human hybrid teleophthalmology: Dow 2023 compared IDx-DR with human teleophthalmology over 2.5 years and evaluated a hybrid workflow combining AI evaluation with expert assessment for referable cases.
- Gradability/nondiagnostic mitigation: Shou 2024 identified predictors of nondiagnostic AI exams pre-reflex dilation to enable a “predictive dilation” workflow, aligning with reflexive dilation used in the pivotal IDx-DR trial. STATUS year-2 (Chen 2025) showed targeted interventions improved AI gradability and encounter volume.
- Patient/provider experience: Nolan 2023 reported rural primary care experiences with EyeArt; Bhambhwani 2025 assessed feasibility, implementation, and patient experience in Northern Ontario.

Clinical impact, adherence, and equity
- Health system adherence/equity (adults): Johns Hopkins (Liu TYA 2024) difference-in-difference analysis showed autonomous AI deployment increased odds of annual diabetic eye exam adherence by 36% vs non-AI sites (2019–2021). Black/African American adherence rose by 11.9% at AI-switched sites vs a 1.2% decrease at non-AI sites; the Asian vs Black/African American adherence gap narrowed from 15.6% to 3.5%, suggesting improved access and equity.
- Youth RCT: ACCESS trial (Wolf 2024) randomized 8–21-year-olds to point-of-care autonomous AI vs referral/education. Eye exam completion within 6 months was 100% (95% CI 95.5–100) with AI vs 22% (95% CI 14.2–32.4) in controls (p<0.001). Among AI-positive participants, 64% completed specialist follow-through vs 22% in controls (p<0.001).

DME detection and treatment
- Autonomous DME detection: Bressler 2025 developed/validated a deep learning model to detect DME from color fundus images across diverse devices/settings for autonomous screening.
- Misclassification analysis: La Franca 2024 quantified ARIAS sensitivity/specificity and misclassification for active DME and identified factors linked to true/false positives.
- Therapy (not screening): Zhang 2025 phase 2 RCT compared RC28-E, a bispecific anti-VEGF-A/FGF-2 antibody, against conbercept in DME, exploring dose/regimen efficacy.

Integrated AI for primary care
