- Endovascular thrombectomy (EVT) for large vessel occlusion (LVO) stroke is highly time dependent; faster workflows correlate with better outcomes. Multiple studies assess artificial intelligence (AI) tools—particularly Viz.ai—for automated LVO detection and coordination to improve time metrics. Related work on Mobile Stroke Units (MSUs) and anesthetic strategies provides broader context on timeliness and workflow.

Key studies and findings

1) Akhter et al., 2025 (retrospective, single comprehensive stroke center; majority Hispanic urban population; 222 EVT patients, 84 pre–AI vs 138 post–AI; Viz LVO implemented July 2020)
- Primary outcome: proportion receiving EVT within 90 minutes of hospital arrival.
- Results:
  - EVT within 90 minutes increased from 26.5% (95% CI 17.9–36.8) to 68.1% (95% CI 60.0–75.5), p < 0.001.
  - Median door-to-groin (DTG) time decreased from 109 to 75 minutes, p < 0.001.
  - Interrupted time-series analysis confirmed significant DTG reduction post-implementation, p = 0.001.
  - A higher percentage of acute ischemic stroke (AIS) patients received EVT post-AI.
  - No significant differences in TICI reperfusion scores, length of stay, or mortality/hospice.
- Conclusion: AI platform (Viz.ai) significantly accelerated in-hospital workflow without measurable change in short-term clinical outcomes at this center.

2) Sarhan et al., 2025 (systematic review and meta-analysis; 12 studies; 15,595 patients; Viz.ai impact)
- Primary workflow outcomes (standardized mean difference, SMD):
  - CT-to-EVT start: SMD -0.71 (95% CI -0.98 to -0.44), p < 0.001.
  - Door-to-groin (DTG): SMD -0.50 (95% CI -0.66 to -0.35), p < 0.001.
  - CT-to-recanalization: SMD -0.55 (95% CI -0.76 to -0.33), p < 0.001.
  - Door-in–door-out (transfer metric): SMD -0.49 (95% CI -0.71 to -0.28), p < 0.001.
- Clinical outcomes: No statistically significant differences in symptomatic ICH, any ICH, mortality, 90-day mRS <2, or length of stay.
- Conclusion: Viz.ai integration consistently improves workflow metrics; effect on clinical outcomes remains unproven, warranting further studies.

3) Ebirim et al., 2025 (JNIS)
- Focus: The magnitude of workflow improvements from automated LVO detection depends on care team engagement and interaction.
- Implication: Real-world gains require effective adoption and communication within stroke teams.

4) Martinez-Gutierrez et al., 2023 (JAMA Neurology; cluster randomized clinical trial)
- Objective: Test whether automated LVO detection software expedites in-hospital workflows for EVT.
- Significance: Provides randomized evidence base for AI-driven workflow interventions; supports the importance of process optimization in time-sensitive stroke care.
- (Specific results not provided in abstract excerpt.)

5) Field et al., 2023
- Finding: AI algorithms aiding LVO identification improve door-to-puncture and interfacility transfer times for EVT candidates.

6) VISIION/ VISIION-S programs (Figurelle et al., 2023; Van Orden et al., 2023)
- Context: Comprehensive Stroke Center and network-level deployment of Viz.ai to streamline workflows and communications.
- Findings: Initial improvements in key performance indicators (KPIs) were demonstrated (VISIION) and subsequently analyzed for sustainability (VISIION-S), indicating maintained benefits over time.
- (Specific metrics not provided in excerpts.)

