- Intraoperative hypotension (IOH), often defined as MAP <65 mmHg, is linked to AKI, myocardial injury (MINS), stroke, delirium, and mortality. The Hypotension Prediction Index (HPI; Edwards Lifesciences) uses arterial waveform features to predict hypotension minutes in advance to enable proactive hemodynamic management. HPI can be derived from invasive arterial lines (Acumen/Acumen IQ on Hemosphere) or the noninvasive ClearSight system. Integration with goal-directed hemodynamic therapy (GDHT) and “smart alerts” may improve protocol adherence.

Evidence from meta-analyses and systematic reviews (2024–2025)
- Ripollés-Melchor et al., 2025 (meta-analysis, major abdominal surgery): Evaluated whether HPI-guided management improves postoperative outcomes; included post hoc analyses of IOH metrics in adults. Focus: outcome impact beyond IOH reduction.
- Liu et al., 2025 (systematic review/meta-analysis): Assessed HPI efficacy for reducing major complications and IOH; notes ongoing controversy about perioperative complication reduction.
- Shirmohamadi et al., 2025 (systematic review/meta-analysis): Appraised HPI for IOH prediction and prevention in cardiac and noncardiac procedures.
- Felippe et al., 2025 (systematic review, meta-analysis, and trial sequential analysis; PROSPERO CRD42024571931): Determined effectiveness of HPI for adult non-cardiac surgery.
- Sriganesh et al., 2024 (systematic review/meta-analysis of RCTs): Evaluated HPI effects on TWA IOH, AUHT, incidence, and duration of hypotension.
- Pilakouta Depaskouale et al., 2024 (systematic review/meta-analysis; “six years of HPI”): Explored whether HPI plus a personalized treatment protocol decreases IOH.
- Broader ML-in-perioperative care: Mehta et al., 2024 and Shimada et al., 2024 conducted systematic reviews/meta-analyses of ML/AI-assisted perioperative interventions, highlighting limited clinical implementation and the need to close research-to-practice gaps.

Randomized and comparative clinical studies
- Prone spine surgery (single-center RCT): HPI-guided vs standard care (n=77 analyzed; 39 HPI). Primary: TWA IOH not significantly different (0.10 mmHg [0.05–0.23] vs 0.15 mmHg [0.09–0.37]; p=0.088). Total hypotension duration per patient lower with HPI (4 min [0.5–12.2] vs 11.2 min [2.6–20.1]; p=0.019). Postop complication rates similar. ClinicalTrials.gov NCT05341167.
- Pancreaticoduodenectomy in elderly (RCT): Tested whether HPI reduces IOH (MAP <65 mmHg). Focus: high-risk abdominal oncology surgery; hypothesis-driven.
- Robotic-assisted radical prostatectomy (RCT): Compared standard goal-directed therapy (GDT) vs HPI-guided GDT to reduce IOH during RARP.
- Cardiac surgery and ICU (HYPE-2 RCT): Investigated whether HPI with diagnostic guidance reduces hypotension during cardiac surgery and ICU stay.
- AI-supported noninvasive continuous monitoring in major orthopedic/trauma surgery (RCT protocol): Tests noninvasive HPI (ClearSight-based) to reduce IOH incidence/severity and related complications, addressing blind gaps of intermittent NIBP.
- Lung surgery pilot (single-lung ventilation): Goal-directed treatment using AI vs standard care to reduce IOH frequency/duration/severity and potentially AKI.
- Head and neck free flap surgery (RCT protocol): Assesses HPI impact on incidence and severity of IOH in long microvascular procedures.
- Major abdominal surgery (RCT protocol): Compares perioperative hypotension rates by monitoring strategy; HPI intended to shift from reactive to proactive management.

Implementation, algorithms, and feasibility
- GDHT compliance: Guerra-Londono et al. feasibility study tested Acumen HPI Smart Alerts and trends to improve compliance with a GDHT algorithm in moderate-to-high-risk elective noncardiac surgery.
- Abdominal aortic aneurysm open repair (multicenter observational; HEMAS): Prospective evaluation of an institutional algorithm including HPI to reduce IOH incidence/severity in high-risk open vascular surgery.

