- Focus: Improving TB case finding via community and facility screening with chest X-ray (CXR), AI/computer-aided detection (CAD) software, and non-sputum triage tests (e.g., C-reactive protein [CRP], host-response assays). Studies span diverse settings (community, prisons, hospitals, migrant screening) and populations (including high TB/HIV burden and vulnerable groups).
- Performance: CAD tools generally show high sensitivity but variable specificity; results are setting- and threshold-dependent. Among evaluated systems, Lunit often shows stronger performance against microbiological reference standards; CAD4TB and Lunit perform well against radiological standards. qXR demonstrates high sensitivity with lower specificity in inpatient triage.
- Operational themes: Mobile CXR can be resource-intensive; combining CAD with point-of-care tests (e.g., CRP) is being tested for effectiveness and cost-effectiveness. Subgroup characteristics and context (e.g., prisons, inpatient care, migrant screening) influence CAD performance and thresholds. WHO triage test TPP is ≥90% sensitivity and ≥70% specificity.
- Evidence gaps: Several protocols and retrospective analyses call for more prospective, large-scale studies, especially on operational performance, cost-effectiveness, and integration with other triage tools.

Key findings by study
- Personnel time requirements for mobile chest X-ray screening for TB (2025): Highlights that community-based active case finding with CXR is effective but constrained by high resource (personnel time) demands; quantifies/characterizes time needs for implementation (details not provided in abstract).

- Community-based screening with CAD vs CAD+CRP in Lesotho/South Africa—paired screen-positive trial protocol (2025): Evaluates effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of CAD-only versus CAD plus point-of-care CRP to address underreporting/underdiagnosis in hard-to-reach, high TB/HIV settings. Outcomes include early detection and linkage to care.

