Key studies and findings

- Efficacy and Safety of iLet Bionic Pancreas in Type 1 Diabetes: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis (PubMed ID: 41157817; DOI: 10.1002/edm2.70127; 2025-01-01)
  • Objective: Evaluate efficacy and safety of the iLet BP, an AID system that doses insulin autonomously using only body weight (no carb counting/user dosing), versus standard care (SC).
  • Design/Scope: Meta-analysis of 5 RCTs totaling 1,130 patients with T1D.
  • Key point: Synthesizes RCT evidence comparing iLet BP to SC; abstract provides design/scale but no pooled outcome metrics.

- Glycaemic Control and Variability with Different HCL Systems in T1D: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of RCTs (PubMed ID: 41024422; DOI: 10.1111/dom.70150; 2025-01-01)
  • Objective: Updated performance analysis of commercially available HCL systems in RCTs involving people with T1D.
  • Design/Scope: Systematic review/meta-analysis; abstract focuses on aim; outcomes not detailed here.

- Randomized Trial of the Insulin-Only iLet BP for Cystic Fibrosis–Related Diabetes (PubMed ID: 37874987; DOI: 10.2337/dc23-1411; 2024-01-01)
  • Objective: Assess safety and efficacy of iLet BP AID in adults with CFRD.
  • Design: Single-center, open-label, random-order, crossover trial in adults with CFRD.
  • Key point: Evaluates iLet BP beyond T1D, in CFRD; abstract provides design but not numeric results.

- Two Years with a Tubeless AID System: Single-Arm Multicenter Trial in Children, Adolescents, and Adults with T1D (PubMed ID: 37850941; DOI: 10.1089/dia.2023.0364; 2024-01-01)
  • Objective/Design: Longitudinal single-arm, multicenter evaluation over two years of a tubeless AID system across ages (children to adults) with T1D.
  • Key point: Long-term, real-world style assessment; abstract text not provided.

- Meta-Analysis of RCT Outcomes for the t:slim X2 Pump with Control-IQ in Ages 2–72 (PubMed ID: 37067353; DOI: 10.1089/dia.2022.0558; 2023-01-01)
  • Objective: Synthesize RCT outcomes for Control-IQ technology across a broad age range (2–72 years).
  • Key point: Comparative benchmark for another widely used HCL system; abstract text not provided.

- Concordance of Central Laboratory HbA1c from Capillary Kits vs Venous Draws in the Insulin-Only BP Pivotal Trial (PubMed ID: 37053531; DOI: 10.1089/dia.2023.0094; 2023-01-01)
  • Objective: Assess accuracy of capillary HbA1c samples versus venous samples.
  • Design/Scope: 773 paired capillary/venous samples from 258 participants; analyzed at a central laboratory.
  • Result: 97.7% of capillary samples were within 5% of their corresponding venous measurements, supporting capillary collection as a viable alternative.

- Utility and Safety of Backup Insulin Regimens Generated by the Bionic Pancreas: Randomized Study (PubMed ID: 36877259; DOI: 10.1089/dia.2022.0461; 2023-01-01)
  • Context: iLet BP initializes with body weight, autonomously doses insulin, and uses qualitative meal announcements; it generates and continuously updates backup insulin regimens (long-acting dose, basal profile, meal doses, correction factor) for use in case of device malfunction.
  • Design: After a 13-week T1D trial, participants aged 6–83 completed 2–4 days with randomized assignments involving their prestudy insulin regimen; abstract is truncated and does not detail outcomes.
  • Key point: Investigates real-world contingency planning via BP-generated backup regimens; safety/utility focus.

- Insulin-Only BP Pivotal Trial Extension Study: Multicenter Single-Arm Evaluation in Adults and Youth with T1D (PubMed ID: 36173238; DOI: 10.1089/dia.2022.0341; 2022-01-01)
