- Early sepsis prediction in the emergency department using machine learning (van Wijk RJ et al., 2025; Am J Emerg Med; DOI: 10.1016/j.ajem.2025.09.034; PubMed: 41027321)
  - Aim: Highlight limitations of qSOFA/NEWS for early sepsis risk and propose ML to integrate diverse data for better early recognition and risk stratification.
  - Key points: Traditional scores require manual estimation and have limited predictive performance. ML can leverage broader, heterogeneous inputs to improve early intervention decisions.

- Interpretable ML to retrospectively identify suspected infection for sepsis surveillance (Tuinte RAM et al., 2025; EClinicalMedicine; DOI: 10.1016/j.eclinm.2025.103401; PubMed: 40823498)
  - Design: Multicentre cohort study.
  - Aim: Operationalize “suspected infection” for sepsis surveillance via an interpretable ML model to retrospectively identify patients with sepsis.
  - Significance: Addresses a key surveillance challenge by providing a transparent, scalable approach.

- Clinical decision support for pediatric septic shock in the ED: cluster RCT (Scott HF et al., 2025; Pediatrics; DOI: 10.1542/peds.2024-069478; PubMed: 40490252)
  - Design: Cluster randomized trial.
  - Hypothesis: ML-based clinical decision support (CDS) would increase the proportion of children receiving septic shock treatment before shock onset.
  - Context: Seeks to reduce preventable mortality from diagnostic delays; tests real-world impact of predictive CDS.

- Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) via ML in sepsis (Ates HC et al., 2024; Cell Reports Medicine; DOI: 10.1016/j.xcrm.2024.101681; PubMed: 39127039)
  - Data source: Randomized controlled trial (DRKS00011159; 10 Oct 2016).
  - Findings: ML identified informative features distinguishing healthy vs sick states; proposed a continuous, data-driven “multidimensional” SOFA-like score.
  - Impact: ML helped uncover links between TDM effectiveness and clinical data; suggests TDM positively influences recovery rates when analyzed with ML.

- Predicting carbapenem-resistant gram-negative bacterial (CRGNB) bloodstream infection in ICU (Liang Q et al., 2024; BMC Med Inform Decis Mak; DOI: 10.1186/s12911-024-02504-4; PubMed: 38745177)
  - Aim: Use ML to predict whether CRGNB cause bloodstream infection in ICU to guide antibiotic selection while awaiting culture results (typically 2–5 days).
  - Rationale: Earlier risk stratification could optimize empiric therapy and antimicrobial stewardship.

- Real-time ML-assisted sepsis alert in ED: cluster RCT (Kijpaisalratana N et al., 2024; Intern Emerg Med; DOI: 10.1007/s11739-024-03535-5; PubMed: 38381351)
  - Setting: Adult ED, tertiary-care hospital; cluster-randomized design.
  - Intervention: Display-screen alerts when ML threshold for sepsis is met, versus conventional (human-initiated) alerts.
  - Sample: 256 intervention vs 318 control sepsis patients.
  - Outcomes:
    - Antibiotics within 1 h: 68.4% vs 60.1% (P=0.04).
    - Antibiotics within 3 h: 94.5% vs 89.0% (P=0.02).
    - Median triage-to-antibiotic time: 46 vs 50 min (marginally shorter in intervention).
    - Diagnostic performance: ML AUROC significantly higher than qSOFA, SIRS, and MEWS.
  - Conclusion: ML alerts can accelerate antibiotic administration and improve diagnostic accuracy vs rule-based systems.
  - Trial registration: Thai Clinical Trials Registry TCTR20230120001 (retrospective).

- Probabilistic graphical models (PGMs) in pediatric sepsis: feasibility and scoping review (Nguyen TM et al., 2023; Transl Pediatr; DOI: 10.21037/tp-23-25; PubMed: 38130578)
  - Aim: Assess feasibility and applications of PGMs for pediatric sepsis and document how pediatric sepsis definitions were used across studies.
