- Paper: Integrated image-based deep learning and language models for primary diabetes care (Li J et al., 2024; DOI: 10.1038/s41591-024-03139-8; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39030266/)
  - System: DeepDR-LLM integrates a large language model (LLM module) with image-based deep learning (DeepDR-Transformer) to support primary care physicians (PCPs) with individualized diabetes management and DR screening.
  - Retrospective evaluation:
    - LLM module performance: Comparable to PCPs and endocrinology residents in English; in Chinese, outperformed PCPs and was comparable to endocrinology residents.
    - Referable DR identification: Average PCP accuracy improved from 81.0% unassisted to 92.3% when assisted by DeepDR-Transformer.
  - Prospective single-center real-world study:
    - Design: Compared unassisted PCP arm (n=397) vs PCP+DeepDR-LLM arm (n=372).
    - Outcomes:
      - Newly diagnosed diabetes: Better self-management behaviors throughout follow-up in the PCP+DeepDR-LLM arm (P<0.05).
      - Patients with referable DR: Higher adherence to DR referrals in the PCP+DeepDR-LLM arm (P<0.01).
      - Quality and empathy of management recommendations improved with DeepDR-LLM.
  - Conclusion: DeepDR-LLM enhanced PCP diagnostic performance, patient self-management, referral adherence, and the quality/empathy of recommendations; promising digital solution for primary diabetes care and DR screening.

- Paper: Autonomous Artificial Intelligence Increases Access and Health Equity in Underserved Populations with Diabetes (Liu TYA et al., 2024; DOI: 10.21203/rs.3.rs-3979992/v1; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38559222/)
  - Background: Diabetic eye disease (DED) is a leading cause of blindness; annual DED testing is sight-saving, cost-effective, and a HEDIS measure, but adherence is historically low, especially among disadvantaged populations. In 2018, the US FDA De Novo cleared autonomous AI for diagnosing DED in primary care.
  - Setting: Johns Hopkins Medicine (JHM), an integrated system with 30+ primary care sites, began deploying autonomous AI for DED testing in some clinics in 2020.
  - Study design: Retrospective difference-in-difference analysis (logistic regression) comparing 2019 vs 2021 adherence changes between:
    - “Non-AI” sites (no AI deployment; standard referral to eyecare) and
    - “AI-switched” sites (no AI in 2019; autonomous AI by 2021).
  - Population: All adult patients with diabetes in the system (2019 cohort n=17,674; 2021 cohort n=17,590); controlled for multiple potential confounders.
  - Key findings:
    - Overall adherence: Odds of adherence at AI-switched sites were 36% higher than at non-AI sites, indicating a greater increase in DED testing post-implementation.
    - Access for disadvantaged groups: For Black/African American patients, adherence increased by 11.9% at AI-switched sites vs decreased by 1.2% at non-AI sites over the same period.
    - Health equity: The adherence gap between Asian Americans and Black/African Americans narrowed from 15.6% (61.1% vs 45.5%) in 2019 to 3.5% in 2021.
  - Conclusion: Autonomous AI deployment improved adherence to a HEDIS measure, increased access, and reduced disparities, particularly benefiting historically underserved populations; calls for prospective validation across more settings.

