AI and imaging/radiomics in acute stroke and neurovascular care
- Systematic review: AI in emergency stroke imaging (9 studies; PRISMA; CLAIM quality assessment; search through Aug 20, 2025)
  - Intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) detection on non-contrast CT: AUC up to 0.98; real-world balanced accuracy ~0.93 with near–real-time processing.
  - Automated ASPECTS: almost perfect agreement with expert consensus (κ up to 0.90); outperformed individual radiologists in hyperacute phase.
  - Ischemic lesion detection (MRI/CTA CNNs): 83–86% accuracy; symmetry-based methods improved detection; limitations in posterior circulation strokes.
  - Prognosis: models integrating imaging + clinical data achieved AUC 0.79–0.91; multimodal deep learning > single-modality or clinical-only.
  - Workflow: AI processing 2–4 minutes; bottlenecks include data transfer/system integration.
  - Interpretability: Grad-CAM and SHAP commonly used, improving transparency.
  - Overall: strong potential for ICH detection, automated ASPECTS, and LVO alerts; multimodal/transformer approaches promising for segmentation and outcome prediction; requires external validation and seamless workflow integration; AI augments rather than replaces clinicians.
- Systematic review/meta-analysis: Viz.ai for emergent LVO detection and workflow (12 studies; 15,595 patients; to Oct 25, 2024)
  - Significant reductions in time metrics:
    - CT-to-EVT start: SMD -0.71 (95% CI -0.98 to -0.44; p<0.001)
    - Door-to-groin: SMD -0.50 (95% CI -0.66 to -0.35; p<0.001)
    - CT-to-recanalization: SMD -0.55 (95% CI -0.76 to -0.33; p<0.001)
    - Door-in–door-out: SMD -0.49 (95% CI -0.71 to -0.28; p<0.001)
  - No statistically significant differences in clinical outcomes (sICH, any ICH, mortality, 90-day mRS<2, length of stay).
  - Conclusion: robust workflow gains; clinical outcome benefit unproven; further studies needed.
- Systematic review: Radiomics and AI in intracranial aneurysm management (84 studies included from 684 screened; most 2018–2024)
  - Use cases: rupture risk prediction (50.0%), diagnosis (25.0%), clinical outcomes (11.9%), growth rate (4.8%), occlusion/stenosis assessment (6.0%), hemodynamics (2.4%).
  - Data types: radiologic only (51.2%), clinical only (28.6%), combined (17.9%), molecular (2.4%).
  - Commercial tools in this space include Aidoc, RapidAI, Viz.AI, and Viz Aneurysm.
  - Field is rapidly evolving with expanding applications across vascular pathologies.

