1) Effectiveness of the GI Genius Computer-Aided Detection System Versus Standard Colonoscopy: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials (PubMed ID: 41246784)
- Design: PRISMA-aligned systematic review and random-effects meta-analysis of RCTs in adults comparing GI Genius-assisted vs standard colonoscopy.
- Search: PubMed, Embase (Ovid), CENTRAL through August 25, 2025; citation chasing.
- Studies/Participants: 7 trials; n = 9,639.
- Device: GI Genius (FDA-cleared CADe) flags suspected polyps in real time.
- Primary outcome:
  - Adenoma detection rate (ADR): RR 1.12 (95% CI 1.03–1.22); I² = 64% (favored GI Genius).
- Secondary outcomes:
  - Polyp detection rate (PDR): 2 trials; n = 5,225; RR 1.05 (95% CI 1.01–1.10); I² = 0% (favored GI Genius).
  - Sessile serrated lesion detection rate (SSLDR): 4 trials; n = 7,013; RR 1.27 (95% CI 1.11–1.47); I² = 11% (favored GI Genius).
  - Lesion counts:
    - Adenomas per colonoscopy/mean adenomas per patient: 6 trials; n = 9,253; SMD 0.15 (95% CI 0.09–0.20); I² = 29% (favored GI Genius).
    - Polyps per colonoscopy/mean polyps: 2 trials; n = 5,228; SMD 0.10 (95% CI 0.05–0.15); I² = 0% (favored GI Genius).
- Advanced adenoma detection rate (advanced ADR): 3 trials; n = 6,328; RR 1.01 (95% CI 0.90–1.13); I² = 6% (no significant difference).
- Safety: Reporting limited; not meta-analyzed.
- Conclusions: GI Genius improves ADR, PDR, SSLDR, and per-procedure lesion counts; no detectable effect on advanced ADR. Supports routine activation during eligible colonoscopies with appropriate team training and consistent response to prompts. Calls for larger multicenter trials with standardized methods and long-term clinical endpoints.
