2025-12-01 10:33:49,543 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a data-backed, side-by-side view of this 55-year-old man’s 10-year cardiovascular risk and how key changes could move the needle. I used two validated risk models and then anchored the “what-if” changes to large meta-analyses so you can see both model-predicted and trial-proven effect sizes.

Profile used for calculations
- Male, 55 years; smoker; total cholesterol 220 mg/dL; HDL 40 mg/dL; systolic BP 150 mmHg; diabetes present; eGFR 60 mL/min/1.73 m²; history of hypertension (treated vs not treated noted below)

Primary 10-year risk estimates (baseline)
- AHA PREVENT equations (2023; includes diabetes and eGFR)
  - 21.7% if not on antihypertensives
  - 26.1% if on antihypertensives (same SBP, but “treated hypertension” carries additional risk in the model)
  - Both fall in the “high” 10-year risk range (≥20%)
  - Model runs used TC 5.69 mmol/L (220 mg/dL) and HDL 1.03 mmol/L (40 mg/dL)
- Framingham risk score (classic Framingham calculator; does not include diabetes in the specific implementation used here)
  - 20.8% if untreated for BP (SBP 150, smoker)
  - 25.7% if treated for BP (SBP 150, smoker)
  - These are close to PREVENT but may underestimate risk because this version did not account for diabetes

What changes most reduce his 10-year risk? (model-based “what-if” scenarios)
Using PREVENT (includes diabetes and eGFR)
- Stop smoking only (same SBP 150): 21.7% → 15.2%
- Lower SBP from 150 to 130 mmHg (treated) while still smoking: 26.1% → 20.9%
- Lower SBP to 120 mmHg (treated) while still smoking: 26.1% → 18.6%
- Improve HDL from 40 to 50 mg/dL (same 150 mmHg, smoker): 21.7% → 20.0%
- Combine: SBP 130 (treated), non-smoker: 20.9% → 14.6%
- Combine more: SBP 120 (treated), non-smoker: 18.6% → 12.9%
- Add lipid improvement consistent with statin therapy (approximate TC 180 mg/dL, HDL 50 mg/dL; statin “on”):
  - SBP 130 (treated), non-smoker, TC 180, HDL 40: 12.3%
  - SBP 120 (treated), non-smoker, TC 180, HDL 50: 9.6%

Cross-check using Framingham (does not include diabetes; changes should be directionally similar but absolute risks may be lower)
- Stop smoking (SBP 150 untreated): 20.8% → 11.0%
- Lower SBP to 130 (treated), still smoking: 25.7% → 21.9%
- Lower SBP to 120 (treated), still smoking: 25.7% → 19.9%
- Raise HDL from 40 to 50 (SBP 150 untreated, smoker): 20.8% → 17.4%
- Combine favorable changes (treated SBP 120, non-smoker, TC 180, HDL 50): 6.4%

How these “what-if” changes align with trial evidence
- Blood pressure lowering
  - Large meta-analysis (123 trials; 613,815 participants): Each 10 mmHg systolic BP reduction reduces major cardiovascular events by ~20% (RR 0.80; 95% CI ~0.77–0.83), coronary heart disease by ~17%, stroke by ~27%, heart failure by ~28%, and all-cause mortality by ~13%, across baseline BP levels and comorbidities.
  - Source: Ettehad et al., Lancet 2016; PubMed 26724178 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26724178/
- Statins (LDL reduction)
  - Meta-analysis of 27 randomized trials: Each 1.0 mmol/L (≈39 mg/dL) LDL-C reduction lowers major vascular events by ~22% (RR ≈0.78), including reductions in coronary death/MI and stroke.
  - Source: Cholesterol Treatment Trialists’ (CTT) Collaboration, Lancet 2012; PMID 22607822 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22607822/
- Smoking cessation
  - In a large cohort analysis, quitting smoking was associated with about a 39% lower CVD risk within 5 years compared with continuing smokers; excess risk persists beyond 5 years but continues to fall over time.
  - Source: Duncan et al., JAMA 2019; PubMed 31429895 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31429895/

Risk category and guideline context
- ACC/AHA prevention guidelines classify 10-year risk ≥20% as “high risk” (for ASCVD) and generally support aggressive risk factor modification at that level.
  - 2019 ACC/AHA Primary Prevention Guideline: Arnett et al., J Am Coll Cardiol 2019; PubMed 30894318 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30894318/
- For adults 40–75 with diabetes, at least moderate-intensity statin therapy is recommended, often high-intensity if multiple risk enhancers are present (e.g., smoking, hypertension, CKD/eGFR 60).
  - 2018 AHA/ACC Cholesterol Guideline: Grundy et al., J Am Coll Cardiol 2019; PubMed 30423393 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30423393/

Key takeaways (numbers you can use)
- Baseline 10-year risk is high: roughly 22–26% by PREVENT (accounts for diabetes and eGFR) and ~21–26% by Framingham (likely an underestimate here because that implementation didn’t include diabetes).
- Single changes help, combinations help most:
  - Stopping smoking alone lowers modeled risk by about 6–10 percentage points (e.g., 21.7% → 15.2% by PREVENT), consistent with ~39% relative risk reduction vs continuing smokers in observational data (JAMA 2019).
  - Lowering SBP from 150 to 120 mmHg lowers modeled risk by ~7–8 percentage points (e.g., 26.1% → 18.6% in PREVENT), consistent with ~20% relative reduction in major CV events per 10 mmHg decrease (Lancet 2016).
  - Adding statin-level lipid lowering can push modeled risk below 10% when combined with smoking cessation and SBP 120 mmHg (PREVENT ~9.6%; Framingham ~6.4% in a non-diabetic model).
- With comprehensive management (stop smoking, SBP ≈120, statin therapy, HDL improvement), the 10-year risk in models falls from high (~22–26%) to intermediate or even low-intermediate (~6–10%), representing a large absolute risk reduction (~12–17 percentage points).

Notes on models and endpoints
- PREVENT equations (AHA, 2023) incorporate diabetes and kidney function (eGFR) and estimate general cardiovascular disease risk.
- Framingham calculators vary by endpoint; the version used here does not include diabetes and likely underestimates absolute risk for this patient. Still, it cross-checks direction and magnitude of change.
- Different tools define “CVD” slightly differently (e.g., inclusion of stroke, heart failure, PAD), so the exact percentages vary; use them to rank and quantify relative benefit, not as exact certainties.

If you want, I can run the Pooled Cohort Equations (ASCVD-specific) as a third check, or tailor combined-scenario estimates to specific LDL or BP targets you’re considering.
