tezolizumab, cobimetinib, and vemurafenib as first-line treatment for unresectable metastatic BRAF V600 mutated melanoma.","authors":"Schmitt AM; Dumas L; Larkin J","abstract":"The treatment of metastatic melanoma has been revolutionized by the introduction of immune checkpoint inhibitors and BRAF/MEK inhibition. Nevertheless, almost half of patients will progress or show primary resistance to treatment. The combination of BRAF/MEK and immune checkpoint inhibition might achieve higher response rates and improve long-term disease control. The IMspire150 trial investigated the combination of atezolizumab, cobimetinib and vemurafenib versus cobimetinib and vemurafenib alone.","doi":"10.1080/14737140.2022.2017286","published_date":"2022-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34904502/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2022-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""},{"paper_id":"34762112","title":"Estimated Cost-effectiveness of Atezolizumab Plus Cobimetinib and Vemurafenib for Treatment of BRAF V600 Variation Metastatic Melanoma.","authors":"Cai C; Yunusa I; Tarhini A","abstract":"In the IMspire150 trial, triplet treatment with atezolizumab and vemurafenib plus cobimetinib significantly improved progression-free survival (PFS) compared with vemurafenib plus cobimetinib alone for treatment of BRAF V600 variation metastatic melanoma. However, considering high cost of this combination, it is unclear if the incremental cost is worth the additional survival benefit.","doi":"10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2021.32262","published_date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34762112/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'BRAF V600E versus V600K immunotherapy outcomes melanoma', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1183 tokens): Summary of included studies on melanoma management (BRAF-mutant focus and immunotherapy)

1) Combination of encorafenib and binimetinib followed by ipilimumab and nivolumab versus ipilimumab and nivolumab in advanced BRAF-mutant melanoma (Robert C et al., Lancet Oncology, 2025; doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(25)00133-0)
- Population: Patients with advanced melanoma harboring BRAF mutations.
- Intervention/comparator: First-line sequencing strategy of targeted therapy (encorafenib + binimetinib) followed by dual checkpoint blockade (ipilimumab + nivolumab) versus upfront ipilimumab + nivolumab alone.
- Rationale: Addresses the optimal first-line sequence for BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma.
- Outcomes/results: Not provided in the excerpt.

2) Effectiveness of anti–PD-1 monotherapy versus anti–PD-1 plus ipilimumab by age (Woo TE et al., Current Oncology, 2023; doi:10.3390/curroncol30100646)
- Population: Younger vs older adults with advanced melanoma.
- Intervention/comparator: Anti–PD-1 monotherapy compared with combination therapy (anti–PD-1 + ipilimumab).
- Aim: Evaluate effectiveness across age groups.
- Outcomes/results: Not provided in the excerpt.

3) Long-term outcomes with dabrafenib monotherapy in BRAF V600–mutant metastatic melanoma (Hauschild A et al., European Journal of Cancer, 2020; doi:10.1016/j.ejca.2019.10.033)
- Data source: Extended follow-up from phase 2 (BREAK-2) and phase 3 (BREAK-3) trials.
- Key finding: Durable outcomes lasting ≥3 years achievable in a subset of patients on dabrafenib monotherapy.
- Note: Additional follow-up needed to fully characterize long-term impact; detailed survival metrics not provided in the excerpt.

4) Extent of surgery for stage III melanoma (Franke V; van Akkooi ACJ, Lancet Oncology, 2019; doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(19)30099-3)
- Topic: Role of completion lymph node dissection (CLND) vs nodal observation after a positive sentinel node.
- Evidence: Two randomized controlled trials did not show improved overall survival with CLND versus observation.
- Nuance: Patients with larger sentinel node metastases (>1 mm) might benefit more from observation than from dissection and could be considered for adjuvant systemic therapy instead of CLND.
- Adjuvant systemic therapy:
  - High-dose ipilimumab improved overall survival.
  - Nivolumab and pembrolizumab improved relapse-free survival with better safety.
  - Dabrafenib + trametinib improved survival outcomes in BRAF-mutant patients.

5) COLUMBUS trial: Overall survival and PFS with encorafenib ± binimetinib vs vemurafenib (Dummer R et al., Lancet Oncology, 2018; doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(18)30497-2)
- Design: Multicentre, open-label, randomized phase 3 trial in BRAF-mutant melanoma.
- Comparison: Encorafenib + binimetinib and encorafenib alone versus vemurafenib.
- Key efficacy: Encorafenib + binimetinib and encorafenib alone improved progression-free survival compared with vemurafenib.
- Overall survival analysis was a study focus; specific OS results not provided in the excerpt.

Cross-study context
- For BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma, targeted therapy with BRAF/MEK inhibitors (encorafenib + binimetinib; dabrafenib ± trametinib) demonstrates meaningful efficacy; durable responses are possible in a subset on dabrafenib monotherapy.
- Immunotherapy with anti–PD-1 agents, alone or combined with ipilimumab, is central in advanced disease; age-related effectiveness is under evaluation (results not provided).
- Optimal sequencing of targeted therapy and immunotherapy is an active question, with a 2025 randomized trial directly comparing upfront ipilimumab + nivolumab versus targeted therapy followed by immunotherapy.
- In stage III disease with positive sentinel nodes, routine CLND does not improve overall survival; emphasis has shifted to nodal observation plus adjuvant systemic therapy. Adjuvant PD-1 inhibitors improve relapse-free survival; high-dose ipilimumab improves overall survival; and adjuvant dabrafenib + trametinib benefits BRAF-mutant 
