Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'coBRIM overall survival update vemurafenib cobimetinib melanoma', 'max_results': 5} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5260 tokens): Here is a concise synthesis of the two items, preserving the key information.

1) coBRIM phase 3 trial update (Lancet Oncology, 2016)
- Citation: Ascierto PA et al. “Cobimetinib combined with vemurafenib in advanced BRAF(V600)-mutant melanoma (coBRIM): updated efficacy results from a randomised, double-blind, phase 3 trial.” DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(16)30122-X. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27480103/
- Study design: Randomised, double-blind, phase 3 trial in previously untreated patients with advanced/metastatic BRAF(V600)-mutant melanoma.
- Arms: Cobimetinib (MEK inhibitor) + vemurafenib (BRAF inhibitor) vs placebo + vemurafenib.
- Previously reported: Combination improved progression-free survival (PFS) over vemurafenib alone.
- This update: Provides longer follow-up including overall survival (OS) and safety, and reports selected biomarker correlative analyses.
- Key takeaway: Confirms clinical benefit of BRAF+MEK inhibition beyond PFS, adds OS/safety context, and explores biomarkers associated with outcomes.

2) Melanoma and Immunotherapy Bridge 2015 (Journal of Translational Medicine, 2016)
- Citation: “Melanoma and immunotherapy bridge 2015: Naples, Italy. 1–5 December 2015.” DOI: 10.1186/s12967-016-0791-2. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27461275/
- Nature: Comprehensive meeting report covering keynote lectures, oral/poster sessions, and cross-disease immunotherapy topics.
- Major thematic areas and highlights:
  Molecular and immuno-advances
  - PI3K/AKT/mTOR activation: immunologic and metabolic consequences in melanoma.
  - Non-mutational adaptive mechanisms driving resistance to BRAF/MEK inhibitors.
  - Tumor-intrinsic β-catenin signaling as a mediator of immune exclusion/avoidance.
  - Intracellular tumor antigens as antibody targets for melanoma immunotherapy.
  Combination therapies
  - Radiotherapy to potentiate immunotherapy.
  - Strategies to induce a T cell–inflamed tumor microenvironment to overcome checkpoint resistance.
  - Biomarkers to guide treatment decisions.
  - Oncolytic therapies combined with checkpoint inhibitors.
  - Combining vs sequencing ipilimumab and PD-1 blockade.
  Clinical trials and updates (selected)
  - BRIM-3: final overall survival for vemurafenib in BRAFV600-mutant metastatic melanoma.
  - Nivolumab + ipilimumab (early-phase concurrent regimen) efficacy/safety update.
  - coBRIM correlative biomarker analysis (cobimetinib + vemurafenib vs placebo + vemurafenib).
  - Atezolizumab (anti–PD-L1) + vemurafenib (Phase Ib): preliminary safety/activity.
  - Epacadostat (IDO1 inhibitor) + pembrolizumab (Phase 1/2): preliminary safety/efficacy.
  - Talimogene laherparepvec (T-VEC) + pembrolizumab (MASTERKEY-265 Phase 1b primary analysis).
  - CheckMate 066: two-year survival update for nivolumab vs dacarbazine; outcomes for patients treated beyond progression (066/067).
  Tumor microenvironment and biomarkers
  - Roles of myeloid cells and tumor-derived exosomes in immunosuppression.
  - SITC biomarker taskforce progress; Immunoscore initiatives and digital pathology advances.
  - New biomarkers of response/resistance to BRAF inhibitors.
  - Chemokine receptor patterns in lymphocytes mirroring metastasis and ipilimumab response.
  - Circulating exosomal PD-1 and CD28 levels pre-ipilimumab correlating with response.
  - Immunological prognostic factors in stage III melanoma.
  Posters and additional studies
  - Mechanisms of resistance to BRAF/MEK inhibition (e.g., mesenchymal-like features, neuregulin-1 upregulation).
  - Metabolic reprogramming and mitochondrial changes in melanoma.
  - Novel therapeutic targets (e.g., PARP inhibition; uPAR antagonists).
  - Toxicity profile of combined BRAF+MEK inhibitors; surgical technique to reduce seroma after node dissection.
  - Biomarker tools: new IHC markers; serum tumor markers; circulating lncRNA HOTAIR as a metastasis-associated marker.
  Other and health policy
  - Sentinel lymph node biopsy timing and melanoma mortality; psyc
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'V600E V600K melanoma targeted therapy outcomes dabrafenib trametinib encorafenib binimetinib', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"31741065","title":"Management of V600E and V600K BRAF-Mutant Melanoma.","authors":"Haugh AM; Johnson DB","abstract":"The optimal management of advanced stage BRAF-mutated melanoma is widely debated and complicated by the availability of several different regimens that significantly improve outcomes but have not been directly compared. While there are many unanswered questions relevant to this patient population, the major uncertainty in current practice is the choice between BRAF/MEK inhibitors or immunotherapy for those with previously untreated metastatic or high-risk disease. Decisions regarding first line therapy should include consideration of patient preference as well as the presence of symptomatic metastatic disease and degree of comorbidity, particularly secondary to any history of severe auto-immune disorder.BRAF/MEK inhibitors have a high response rate and rapid onset and thus can be quickly introduced when patients are symptomatic. They have also produced long-term responses in a subset of patients with more favorable prognostic indicators. In addition, impressive survival benefits have also been observed in patients with resected stage 3 disease at high risk of recurrence. On the other hand, anti-PD-1 monotherapy is associated with high rates of clinical benefit (~45% response rate in the metastatic setting) and low rates of severe toxicity. In many patients with adverse prognostic features, we use combined anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 for metastatic disease. While associated with high rates of toxicity, adverse events are largely manageable with corticosteroids and treatment cessation, in which case patients may continue to benefit even after a limited duration of treatment.Multiple treatment options exist for patients with BRAF V600 mutant melanoma. Herein, we review the clinical data for safety and efficacy of these options.","doi":"10.1007/s11864-019-0680-z","published_date":"2019-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31741065/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2019-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'V600E V600K melanoma immunotherapy outcomes PD-1 ipilimumab nivolumab pembrolizumab', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"37887546","title":"Effectiveness of Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor with Anti-PD-1 Monotherapy or in Combination with Ipilimumab in Younger versus Older Adults with Advanced Melanoma.","authors":"Woo TE; Stukalin I; Ding PQ; Goutam S; Sander M; Ewanchuk B; Cheung WY; Heng DYC; Cheng T","abstract":null,"doi":"10.3390/curroncol30100646","published_date":"2023-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37887546/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2023-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""},{"paper_id":"31741065","title":"Management of V600E and V600K BRAF-Mutant Melanoma.","authors":"Haugh AM; Johnson DB","abstract":"The optimal management of advanced stage BRAF-mutated melanoma is widely debated and complicated by the availability of several different regimens that significantly improve outcomes but have not been directly compared. While there are many unanswered questions relevant to this patient population, the major uncertainty in current practice is the choice between BRAF/MEK inhibitors or immunotherapy for those with previously untreated metastatic or high-risk disease. Decisions regarding first line therapy should include consideration of patient preference as well as the presence of symptomatic metastatic disease and degree of comorbidity, particularly secondary to any history of severe auto-immune disorder.BRAF/MEK inhibitors have a high response rate and rapid onset and thus can be quickly introduced when patients are symptomatic. They have also produced long-term responses in a subset of patients with more favorable prognostic indicators. In addition, impressive survival benefits have also been observed in patients with resected stage 3 disease at high risk of recurrence. On the other hand, anti-PD-1 monotherapy is associated with high rates of clinical benefit (~45% response rate in the metastatic setting) and low rates of severe toxicity. In many patients with adverse prognostic features, we use combined anti-PD-1 and anti-CTLA-4 for metastatic disease. While associated with high rates of toxicity, adverse events are largely manageable with corticosteroids and treatment cessation, in which case patients may continue to benefit even after a limited duration of treatment.Multiple treatment options exist for patients with BRAF V600 mutant melanoma. Herein, we review the clinical data for safety and efficacy of these options.","doi":"10.1007/s11864-019-0680-z","published_date":"2019-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31741065/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2019-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'COMBI-MB intracranial response dabrafenib trametinib melanoma brain metastases', 'max_results': 5} on Paper Search succeeded. Result: [{"paper_id":"36477181","title":"Clinical Features Associated with Outcomes and Biomarker Analysis of Dabrafenib plus Trametinib Treatment in Patients with BRAF-Mutant Melanoma Brain Metastases.","authors":"Wilmott JS; Tawbi H; Engh JA; Amankulor NM; Shivalingam B; Banerjee H; Vergara IA; Lee H; Johansson PA; Ferguson PM; Saiag P; Robert C; Grob JJ; Butterfield LH; Scolyer RA; Kirkwood JM; Long GV; Davies MA","abstract":"This study aimed to identify baseline clinical features associated with the outcomes of patients enrolled in the COMBI-MB phase II study of dabrafenib and trametinib treatment in patients with V600 BRAF-mutant metastatic melanoma with melanoma brain metastases (MBM). Exploratory biomarker analysis was also conducted as part of the synergistic COMBI-BRV trial (BRV116521), to identify molecular and immunologic changes associated with dabrafenib in MBMs and extracranial metastases (ECM).","doi":"10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-22-2581","published_date":"2023-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36477181/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2023-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""},{"paper_id":"33587894","title":"Circulating tumour DNA in patients with advanced melanoma treated with dabrafenib or dabrafenib plus trametinib: a clinical validation study.","authors":"Syeda MM; Wiggins JM; Corless BC; Long GV; Flaherty KT; Schadendorf D; Nathan PD; Robert C; Ribas A; Davies MA; Grob JJ; Gasal E; Squires M; Marker M; Garrett J; Brase JC; Polsky D","abstract":"Melanoma lacks validated blood-based biomarkers for monitoring and predicting treatment efficacy. Cell-free circulating tumour DNA (ctDNA) is a promising biomarker; however, various detection methods have been used, and, to date, no large studies have examined the association between serial changes in ctDNA and survival after BRAF, MEK, or BRAF plus MEK inhibitor therapy. We aimed to evaluate whether baseline ctDNA concentrations and kinetics could predict survival outcomes.","doi":"10.1016/S1470-2045(20)30726-9","published_date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33587894/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""},{"paper_id":"28592387","title":"Dabrafenib plus trametinib in patients with BRAF","authors":"Davies MA; Saiag P; Robert C; Grob JJ; Flaherty KT; Arance A; Chiarion-Sileni V; Thomas L; Lesimple T; Mortier L; Moschos SJ; Hogg D; Márquez-Rodas I; Del Vecchio M; Lebbé C; Meyer N; Zhang Y; Huang Y; Mookerjee B; Long GV","abstract":"Dabrafenib plus trametinib improves clinical outcomes in BRAF","doi":"10.1016/S1470-2045(17)30429-1","published_date":"2017-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28592387/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2017-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
