  - Capture key operational metadata per endpoint: required vs optional params, value ranges and defaults (e.g., limit and PageSize), pagination model, enums, content types, and notable headers.
- Validate auth quickly with curl against one read-only endpoint for each API:
  - Stripe example (expect 200 with valid key; 401 without):
    - curl https://api.stripe.com/v1/customers -H "Authorization: Bearer sk_test_..." -d limit=1
    - Docs: https://stripe.com/docs/api/authentication
  - Twilio example (expect 200 with valid Basic; 401 without):
    - curl -u "ACxxxx:your_auth_token" https://api.twilio.com/2010-04-01/Accounts.json?PageSize=1
    - Or with API Keys (recommended): curl -u "SKxxxx:your_api_key_secret" https://api.twilio.com/2010-04-01/Accounts.json?PageSize=1
    - Docs: https://www.twilio.com/docs/iam/keys/api-keys
- Present the comparison with concrete items:
  - Auth scheme: Stripe Bearer vs Twilio Basic (Account SID/Auth Token or API Key SID/Secret).
  - Endpoint count and base URLs (Stripe 585 at api.stripe.com; Twilio 197 at api.twilio.com; Twilio Messaging 57 at messaging.twilio.com).
  - Pagination defaults and ranges (Stripe default limit 10; Twilio Messaging default PageSize 50 up to 1000 on the endpoints cited).
  - Notable operational details per endpoint that impact integration (e.g., Stripe expand[], created range filters; Twilio Messaging ValidityPeriod default 36,000 seconds; explicit CORS headers documented on some Twilio endpoints).
  - Webhook verification mechanisms for both vendors (header names and doc links).

